            },
            {
                "entry_id": "14f754a3-b8bc-3f0d-8c89-d234a9f7260d",
                "word": "blighty",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "b30af5df-dd06-40cd-9bc0-8a84b75fa87d",
                        "entry_id": "14f754a3-b8bc-3f0d-8c89-d234a9f7260d",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "blighties"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "86df0d75-79d3-33b8-8bd9-319c58e95df3",
                        "entry_id": "14f754a3-b8bc-3f0d-8c89-d234a9f7260d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Alternative form of Blighty."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "alt-of",
                            "alternative"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c0980994-0119-4170-abb3-14fadc5a3f32",
                                "sense_id": "86df0d75-79d3-33b8-8bd9-319c58e95df3",
                                "text": "With such delights awaiting them only a few miles to the rear, it's no wonder that men prayed for a “blighty” – a small wound that would not incapacitate them for life, but would get them out of the line for a month or even a week. . . . a stray bullet pierced Moore's left foot. Moore cried out, not with pain, but with delight. ¶ “Oh,” he shouted, “it's a beauty, Vic! What a present from the Red Devil! It's a Blighty, I'll bet a dollar.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "642d5cba-8658-42c3-bcd5-ecccb1ede211",
                                "sense_id": "86df0d75-79d3-33b8-8bd9-319c58e95df3",
                                "text": "And after a short time with the West Yorkshires I came to see that discipline and spirit, even at my platoon level, led to fewer cases of minor ailments like colds and flu, to a substantial reduction in trench foot and to the total absence of suicides or deliberately provoked blighties."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d34fcb99-b9a0-4db1-b4a9-06f41a8a26da",
                                "sense_id": "86df0d75-79d3-33b8-8bd9-319c58e95df3",
                                "text": "'I don't want any of these blighties', Len Wilton concluded."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
